    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.extra\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12.x\n\n### Description\n\nUsing the `gen_ai_spec.indent()` textobject from `mini.extra` seems to use charwise `vis_mode`, which breaks the sorting operator provided by `mini.operators`. I think this is because `MiniOperators.sort()` behaves differently based on the mode. Using `gsVii` doesn't have this problem.\nThe textobject provided by `mini.indentscope` works correctly, but I'm not a fan of the visual guides so I prefer to just use the one provided by `mini.extra`.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n`init.lua`:\n```lua\nvim.o.shiftwidth = 2\nvim.o.tabstop = 2\nvim.o.expandtab = true\nvim.pack.add({ \"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim\" })\nrequire(\"mini.operators\").setup()\nrequire(\"mini.ai\").setup({\n  custom_textobjects = {\n    i = require(\"mini.extra\").gen_ai_spec.indent(),\n  },\n})\n```\nExample file:\n```lua\nlocal foo = {\n  'aaa',\n  'ccc',\n  'bbb',\n}\n```\nType `gsii` with cursor inside indent:\n```lua\nlocal foo = {\n  ,\n  'aaa',\n  'bbb','ccc'\n}\n```\nExpected result:\n```lua\nlocal foo = {\n  'aaa',\n  'bbb',\n  'ccc',\n}\n```",
